{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 psychological drama film directed by david fincher, written by grant heslov, ben richardson, and cynthia mort, and produced by fincher, heslov, richardson, and mort. the film is based on the real-life story of the founding of mark zuckerberg and his creation of the social networking site facebook.\n\nthe film begins with mark zuckerberg (played by jesse eisenberg) meeting his future college roommate and eventual business partner, eduardo saverin (played by andrew garfield), at harvard university. the story follows their journey as they create facebook, initially called \"thefacebook,\" and take it to the masses.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its portrayal of the complex and often tumultuous relationship between zuckerberg and saverin. their friendship and partnership are tested as facebook gains popularity, and the duo's differing views on the direction of the company create tension.\n\nthe film also explores the role of sean parker (played by jason sekelman) and dustin moskovitz (played by jason torres easterly), two other key figures in the creation of facebook. parker serves as the company's first chief technology officer, while moskovitz handles the business side of things.\n\nthe film features a talented ensemble cast, with standout performances from eisenberg as zuckerberg and garfield as saverin. the supporting cast, including dustin hoffman as napster's founder sean fanning, added depth and complexity to the story.\n\nthe cinematography and score in the film are also noteworthy. david fincher's direction brings a sense of tension and unease to the film, while trent reznor and atticus ross's score perfectly complements the mood and tone of each scene.\n\nthe film received widespread critical acclaim upon its release, with many praising its thoughtful and nuanced portrayal of the facebook phenomenon. 